  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cold front passes through the region Sunday \nmorning with conditions improving for Sunday afternoon.\n\nA weak disturbance across Delmarva will continue to move offshore\ninto the evening. Scattered showers have accompanied it so far.\nA rumble of thunder across far south Delaware is possible\nhowever. \n\nA low pressure system along with a negatively tilted H5 trough \nacross the Great Lakes attm will move E-NE towards southern Canada\nby Sunday. An associated cold front will move across the Middle\nAtlantic region Sunday morning and then offshore by early afternoon.\nWhile the front will be crossing through at a time when heating\nwill be at a minimum, we still have some good dynamics and a humid\nairmass in place. Accordingly, we expect showers to increase over\nthe area later tonight and persist until FROPA on Sunday. Pops\nare Likely/Categorical for the Wester/Northern counties and\nChance for areas South/East. \n\nThe threat for Excessive Rainfall seems to overall be rather low\nwith ample storm motion expected to keep the soakers from stalling\nacross one area. Most areas will see one-quarter to one-half inch\nof rain with a few isolated spots perhaps getting around an inch.\nThe higher totals will likely be across the Southern Poconos and\nNorth NJ. \n\nSevere weather remains possible and with CAPE values increasing\novernight and peaking before FROPA Sunday, we could see some instances\nof Severe weather, Again, the favored areas will be North/Northwest\nwhere the SPC has kept the MARGINAL tag for the updated Day2 Outlook.\nDamaging winds are the primary threat for early Sunday.\n\nThe front passes across the area Sunday morning while the best\ndynamics exit to the Northeast. Rains and thunder will end from\nWest to East during the morning and fair weather should push in\nfor the afternoon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Fair weather is expected to return later Sunday\nand should persist into the middle of the upcoming week. \n\nMore zonal flow with some subtle ridging moves in for the first\nhalf of the week. At the surface, high pressure will mainly be \nin control, resulting in dry and tranquil weather through \nWednesday. Temperatures should be seasonable through this \nstretch.",
